#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""claude-session-mover (csm): move a Claude Code session to another of your own Claude accounts.

    csm accounts                           # the config homes on this machine, and who is signed in
    csm list                               # recent sessions in every account, newest first
    csm list --account work --json
    csm move 3 --to work                   # row 3 of the last `csm list`, to the account "work"
    csm move 3 --to work --stop-source     # ...and stop the old process once the new one is up
    csm move "refactor" --to personal --dry-run
    csm move https://claude.ai/code/session_01Ab... --to work
    csm stop 3                             # end a session csm moved, and free its window
    csm matrix                             # what your accounts share, and what they cannot
    csm matrix --check                     # ...as a check: exit 1 when a shared layer drifted
    csm --ssh otherhost list               # the same command on another machine

WHY: one Claude account runs out of quota while your others have plenty. Starting the work
over in another account loses the conversation. A move keeps it: the agent remembers
everything, and every turn from then on is spent on the other account.

AN ACCOUNT is a Claude Code config home, the directory CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR points at:
`~/.claude` is the account "default" (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR unset) and every `~/.claude-<name>`
directory is the account "<name>". Sign one in once with `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude-<name>
claude`, then `/login`. An accounts file replaces discovery (see `csm accounts --help`).

🛑 A MOVE IS BETWEEN TWO CONFIG HOMES ON ONE MACHINE, never between machines: a transcript
lives under `<home>/projects/`, which is per machine, and `--resume` looks only inside the
home it is running under. `--ssh HOST` runs this same command ON that machine, where it
again moves between two homes there.

A MOVE IS A COPY AND A RESUME:
  1. the transcript `<home>/projects/<folder>/<id>.jsonl` and its sibling `<id>/` directory
     are copied into the target home, under the source's own folder name, together with the
     session's own side data outside `projects/` (file-history, task list) when it has any;
  2. the target home's first-run gates (workspace trust for that directory, and onboarding)
     are pre-answered in its state file, additively, atomically, backed up;
  3. `claude --resume <id> --remote-control=<title>` starts in a detached tmux window
     `moved-<id8>` of the tmux session `csm-<account>`, under the target's CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR,
     in the session's recorded working directory, with a scrubbed environment;
  4. the tool waits for the window to print its claude.ai link, and only then (with
     --stop-source) stops the old process.
The CLI connects as the target login and registers a NEW claude.ai session owned by it.
The source transcript is never edited or deleted.

WHAT A MOVE COSTS, because the account is the payment layer and little else:
  * preserved thinking is bound to the organization that wrote it, so across organizations
    the CLI drops it and the first turn RE-READS the session — on the new account's quota.
    Move early, while there is quota to spare, not at the limit;
  * the moved session gets a NEW claude.ai link owned by the target account; the old link
    still opens the source account's copy;
  * claude.ai connectors belong to the ACCOUNT and MCP sign-ins to the config home, so the
    moved session gets the target's, not the source's;
  * plugins are installed per home, even when one shared settings file enables them for all;
  * the prompt cache starts cold, and the copied transcript still names the source account
    inside itself until the conversation's context is rebuilt.
`csm move` prints the ones that apply to the move in front of you.

SAFETY, in one place: nothing is written before every refusal has been checked; the source
transcript is never edited or deleted; the session running the tool is recognised and never
stopped; a process is signalled only after its identity is PROVED (pid plus start time);
`csm stop` ends only a window csm itself opened; tmux targets are exact-matched; every
credential is probed for existence only, never read, printed or logged; and the child's
environment is scrubbed of the whole CLAUDE*/ANTHROPIC* families.

Everything this tool reads is Claude Code's own undocumented on-disk state (measured on CLI
2.1.296-2.1.300 on macOS). docs/design.md has the design, the formats and the measurements;
the tests in tests/ pin every shape the tool depends on.
"""

